Research digest for dmitrii
2026-11-03 to 2026-11-16 (inclusive, UTC)
Categories: cs.LG, cs.CL, stat.ML, cs.AI, cs.IR

15 recommended papers

1. The Behavioral Rate of Fine-Tuning: Compression as a Measure of Useful Information
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06598v1

Defines a behavioral compression rate for LoRA adapters based on retained held-out fine-tuning gain. Reports that compressibility depends on the frozen base model and that compression can filter some damage from corrupted training data.

Why it matters to you: Directly connects low-rank compressibility, singular directions, model quality, and noisy-data behavior.

2. Spectral and Subspace Dynamics of LoRA in Supervised Fine-Tuning and Reinforcement Learning
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08414v1

Tracks LoRA spectra and subspace rotation in supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning. Controlled comparisons identify group-mean baseline subtraction as a source of their different dynamics.

Why it matters to you: Closely matches the interest in LoRA geometry, effective rank, and practical language-model fine-tuning.

3. From What Is Used to What Is Missing: Rethinking Dynamic Rank in LoRA
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07349v1

Proposes GEAR, which uses information outside the current LoRA tangent space to guide rank expansion. On two language-model backbones, it reports higher average commonsense-reasoning scores than the compared methods with fewer trainable parameters.

Why it matters to you: Addresses the geometry and optimization of dynamically ranked LoRA updates. Its additional probing and audit costs matter when assessing efficiency.

4. ResQAdam: Residual-Preserving Adaptive Optimization for Memory-Efficient Quantization Aware Training
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08924v1

Proposes an optimizer for quantization-aware LLM training that preserves a signed residual outside the principal low-rank subspace. In its reported hardware comparison, optimizer-state memory falls by about 71–73%, total peak memory by 15–20%, and throughput by less than 2% relative to AdamW.

Why it matters to you: Provides measured quality, memory, and throughput tradeoffs for low-rank optimizer-state compression.

5. Finite Spectral Depth Controls Gaussian Entrance Rank and Avoidance of the First Gradient-Flow Saddle
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05022v1

Theoretically studies finite Newton–Schulz depth in matrix-factorization dynamics and shows that it can change early rank selection and bypass a gradient-flow stationary point.

Why it matters to you: Directly connects spectral optimizer implementation choices to learned rank.

6. DisMUSE: An Efficient Distributed Singular-Spectrum-Based Optimization Approach with Theoretical Convergence Guarantee
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10212v1

Introduces a distributed Muon-style spectral optimizer with variance-reduced momentum, a stated convergence rate, and a low-rank momentum-transmission extension.

Why it matters to you: Matches spectral matrix optimization and the convergence–communication tradeoff.

7. Spectral Rank and Step-Direction Dynamics in Language-Model Post-Training
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.11752v1

Shows why low stable rank need not imply that a language-model update is well approximated at low rank. Selected OLMo-2-7B PPO attention updates require hundreds of singular components to capture 80% of their energy.

Why it matters to you: Challenges simple rank proxies used to judge update compressibility.

8. Delayed Effects of Temporary Weight Decay in Muon Pretraining
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17876v1

Studies temporary weight decay during Muon pretraining across four Transformer sizes and reports delayed, scale-dependent effects on validation loss.

Why it matters to you: Informs optimization and regularization choices across model scales, though the supplied evidence does not establish effects on weight compressibility.

9. Grokking Can Be Steered: Selecting Desirable Solutions By Choice Of Learning Rule
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.19477v1

On modular addition, studies how learning-rule choices alter the size of circuits implementing a generalizing solution at similar accuracy.

Why it matters to you: Connects optimization, memorization-to-generalization transitions, and compressibility, albeit outside LLM pretraining.

10. SaMA: Morpho Adaptation via Asymmetric Expansion of Kronecker Product
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04462v1

Proposes a parameter-efficient alternative to LoRA motivated by factorization geometry and effective-rank scaling. The abstract reports gains over PEFT baselines on reasoning benchmarks.

Why it matters to you: Useful for comparing low-rank parameterizations and their optimization geometry.

11. MemCredit: From Relevance to Utility for Decision-Time Memory in Long-Horizon Web Agents
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.15600v1

Proposes selecting an agent's historical memories by estimated decision utility after semantic retrieval generates candidates.

Why it matters to you: May inform context selection in a research-digest agent, although it evaluates long-horizon agents rather than paper digests.

12. Train High, Ship Low: Efficient LoRA Synchronization for Distributed On-Policy Post-Training
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13306v1

Separates LoRA training rank from synchronization cost using compressed gradient dissemination and local optimizer replay. Reports reduced adapter-byte transmission and faster updates under bandwidth constraints.

Why it matters to you: Addresses quality and communication tradeoffs in distributed low-rank post-training.

13. Direction or Magnitude? Learning Scalars over Task-Calibrated Directions
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.17784v1

Introduces a scalar-coefficient adaptation method and reports a comparison with LoRA on trainable parameters, peak memory, and throughput.

Why it matters to you: Offers a practical PEFT efficiency comparison, though it is not a LoRA optimization method.

14. Regularization Amplifies Subliminal Learning
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10397v1

Reports that low-rank adaptation and other regularization can strengthen covert bias transfer in the studied settings.

Why it matters to you: Highlights a behavioral consequence of constrained fine-tuning that parameter-count and task-accuracy comparisons may miss.

15. DictLLM: Post-training Compression of Large Language Model with Dictionary Kernels
https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08082v1

Proposes post-training LLM compression using shared SVD-derived dictionaries, sparse layer-specific coefficients, and quantization.

Why it matters to you: Provides a weight-compression method relevant to assessing low-rank compressibility, but does not test whether training itself improves compressibility.

Limitations:
- Most selections were supported only by supplied abstracts or truncated excerpts; their baselines, controls, and measured costs were not independently inspected.
- Code availability and reproducibility were not verified.
- The GEAR language-model comparison reports unequal trainable-parameter counts; the supplied measured runtime and memory comparison is on a vision task.
- No X attention analysis was available or used.
- inspect:2611.08924: only question-relevant HTML passages were inspected, not the whole paper.
